Q: Unity Action decouple classes A: <p>If you are using a static event you cannot really have much of an abstraction layer.</p>
<p>Static events are also a form of a global variable, so every downside of mutable global state applies to static events. So I would only recommend this approach if you are absolutely sure that there will only ever be one of these events.</p>
<p>The standard approach is to use avoid static variables and instead use dependency injection, i.e. something like this:</p>
<pre><code>interface IClickInterface
{
    event Action OnClickControllerEvent;
}
public class ClickController : MonoBehaviour, IClickInterface
{
    public event Action OnClickControllerEvent; 
    private void OnLeftClick()
    {
        ...
        OnClickControllerEvent?.Invoke()
    }
}

public class HealthController(IClickInterface clickController) : MonoBehaviour
{
    ...
    private void OnEnable()  =&gt; clickController.OnClickControllerEvent += TakeDamage;
    private void OnDisable()  =&gt; clickController.OnClickControllerEvent -= TakeDamage;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This approach allow you to replace <code>ClickController</code> with some other class if you need to.</p>
<p>You might also consider using a dependency injection container to simplify the creation of all your objects. See <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/extensions/dependency-injection/overview" rel="nofollow noreferrer">.Net dependency injection</a> for one example of a DI container.</p>
